   "notes": "Every species §50-101(d) and (f) name, and every genus it lists whole: dodder (Cuscuta spp.), Rumex spp., witchweed (Striga spp.), Crotalaria spp., cocklebur (Xanthium spp.), and the thistle genera it names (Carduus, Cirsium, Onopordum, Silybum, Scolymus, Salsola), natives in them too: the rule takes them in. Lythrum is the whole genus and its hybrids by §50-709 (purple loosestrife is on (d) and (f) too: shown by §50-709, which says more). Read narrower than printed, where a common name says which plants are meant: \"wild onion and/or wild garlic (Allium spp.)\" as wild onion, Allium canadense, and wild garlic, Allium vineale; \"alligatorweed (Alternanthera spp.)\" as alligatorweed, A. philoxeroides. Left out: \"morning glory (Ipomoea spp.)\", which names no species, and read whole would make the sweet potato a noxious weed; the thistle entry's \"and other genera\", which names none; red rice, \"Oryza sativa var.\", which names no variety (the species is rice).",
